{"item_id":"POST-ef9d64ce0a592e57835f4377-1","title":"What bounced addresses are really telling you","body":"A bounced address is usually not the real problem. It is a signal that something in the workflow is leaking: a typo at capture, stale CRM data, a bad import, or a contact list nobody owns closely enough. For a small business, the real decision is not whether to “use AI,” but whether this is a repeatable email and CRM cleanup problem or just occasional noise that a person can clear faster than any system.

That distinction matters. If bounces happen often enough to block replies, waste follow-up, or leave records drifting out of date, you have a bounded workflow worth mapping. If they do not, the fix may be simpler: better forms, a clearer list owner, or one manual review step. AI should not be asked to be heroic in a vague mess. It rarely gets a medal.

The practical next step is simple: take one week of bounced messages and trace each one back to its source. Note whether it came from a form, an import, a sales reply, or an old record. Then ask one question: would a human need to decide what to retry, suppress, or investigate? If yes, that is a clean diagnostic for a controlled automation discussion. If no, you probably need cleaner input, not more tooling. Human approval should stay on list changes either way."}
